Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The tragedy of the commons, which occurs when there is a public good that is not administered by anyone, cannot be solved through moral suasion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Moral suasion is a common tactic used to attempt to prevent the tragedy of the commons, though it does not work.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>First, when moral suasion is attempted, in the mind of the intended target, they can choose to either</p><ol><li>Ignore the moral suasion, and live with shame, or</li><li>Follow along, but watch as others continue to exploit the commons.</li></ol><p>This results in cognitive dissonance that can confuse or disorientate people. In addition, conscience is self-eliminating. That is, those that do not exploit a commons may be less successful than those that do, eventually leading those who do not exploit the commons to be out-competed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a><ul><li>If this is the case on a somewhat large scale, then would problems of the commons become much less problematic?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Selective incentives are excludable benefits or sanctions that groups use to overcome the free-rider problem\">Selective incentives are excludable benefits or sanctions that groups use to overcome the free-rider problem</a><ul><li>Incentives solve the problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are three different types of chemical bonds: covalent, ionic, and metallic. However, only covalent and ionic are relevant for biology.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Chemical bonds, along with Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules intermolecular forces, are important in biology.</p><h4>Bonds</h4><p>There are three types of chemical bonds:</p><ul><li>Covalent bonds share electrons </li><li>Ionic bonds transfer electrons</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules\">Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules</a></li></ul>","CHONPSNaCaK are the most fundamental building blocks of biological molecules":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>CHONPSNaCaK are the molecules that are the most fundamental building blocks of life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These molecules are an important aspect of many of the macromolecules that make up life.</p><h4>What Does It Stand For?</h4><ol><li>Carbon</li><li>Hydrogen</li><li>Oxygen</li><li>Nitrogen</li><li>Phosphorus</li><li>Sulfur</li><li>Sodium</li><li>Calcium</li><li>Potassium</li></ol><h4>Notes</h4><p>See Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds which indicates how the molecules described here could bond with other molecules.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds\">Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds</a></li></ul>","Cholesterol is used to control the fluidity of membranes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the shape of cholesterol, it is able to fit into gaps in the phospholipid membrane to ensure the right level of structure/fluidity in the membrane.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains one of functions of cholesterol, which is relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cholesterol is the starting material for the synthesis of many hormones\">Cholesterol is the starting material for the synthesis of many hormones</a></li></ul>","Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Apparent disagreements about whether we have free will typically resolve into disagreements about what control condition moral responsibility requires, so the metaphysical dispute is downstream of an ethical one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This reframes the whole debate: one cannot settle whether free will exists without first fixing what it is needed for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The point has a deflationary consequence. If free will just is whatever control condition responsibility requires, then two parties who agree on all the facts about causation can still disagree about free will, because they are disagreeing about desert.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences\">Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences</a><ul><li>The strongest version of the control condition at issue.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Retributive punishment is never justified\">Retributive punishment is never justified</a><ul><li>What follows if the required control condition is never satisfied.</li></ul></li></ul>","Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something that is repeatedly observable when viewing the historical process by which institutions were created is that often, new institutions created in order to rectify an existing flaw serves merely to reproduce the flaw in a different location.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the futility of attempting to create new institutions in order to solve fundamental flaws.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When institutions are created with the purpose of making up for some flaw within existing institutions, it is likely that they themselves will merely reproduce the flaws downstream. For example, the censorate of the Chinese civil service exams were designed to be able to catch corruption in the system and prevent unfairness in the exam system. However, it itself become an instrument of corruption and court politics.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>Part of the reason why institutions designed to solve a problem often don’t.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>So if they fail to solve the problem, it can lead to the creation of new institutions to solve the unsolved problems, as the size of the government drastically grows.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>One way a flaw in an institution could show itself.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient\">The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient</a><ul><li>Note regarding the example about the imperial civil service exams.</li></ul></li></ul>","Many aspects of Buddhism derive from Hinduism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are many similarities between Buddhism and Hinduism, and that is largely because many elements of Buddhism are derived from Hinduism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains many of the origins of the practices from Buddhism that are reminiscent of Hinduism. It also explains why Buddhism made such a large impact when it was introduced in India.</p><h4>Buddhism &amp; Hinduism</h4><ul><li>Many aspects of Buddhism were derived from Hinduism, and Buddhism can be seen as a simplified and standardized version of Hinduism.</li><li>At the same time, Buddhism challenged the inequalities that were introduced by a Hindu caste system, arguing that there are no barriers to enlightenment.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there\">Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD\">The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD</a></li></ul>","Modesty is not a virtue; rather, it is a feeling":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle says that modesty is not a virtue. Rather, it is a feeling, something that is readily mutable, rather than a disposition or a state. He defines it as the “fear of disrepute.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Normally, modesty is defined as a virtue. Aristotle, however, defines it a feeling. He also has some very specific thoughts regarding the nature of modesty, viz. who it is good for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that modesty is good for young people, but bad for virtuous adults. Regarding virtuous adults, he makes the argument that:</p><ol><li>Virtuous people does not do shameful things.</li><li>You only feel modesty (shame, as defined by Aristotle) when you are shameful.</li><li>$\\therefore$ Virtuous people do not feel modesty.</li></ol><p>Virtuous people only “feel” modesty “in hypothesis,” in other words, they can imagine themselves feeling shame if they were to do something not virtuous. In young people, however, since they live by “passion” rather than “reason,” they are prone to making mistakes due to their inexperience. Then, having the modesty to admit their mistakes and change themselves becomes a boon to their development. Modesty is better than shamelessness, but still not as good as not being modest (for the adult).</p><h4>References</h4>","Only around the beginning of modernity have people begun to see romantic love as something that makes life worthy and significant":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As the values that define our lives begin to fade during the Enlightenment, people sought new ways to define meaning in life. One form of this was the emergence of romantic satisfaction as a means of finding meaning in life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the important that our society places upon romantic affection, since it is a means by which people find meaning and satisfaction in their existence.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Anthony Giddens, <em>The Transformation of Intimacy</em></p><p>The other, by being who he or she is, answers a lack… And this lack is directly to do with self-identity: in some sense, the flawed individual is made whole.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a><ul><li>This could potentially be related to the ideas discussed in this note, that a sense of meaninglessness pervaded public sentiment during and after the Enlightenment, leading people to seek new ways to find meaning in life.</li></ul></li></ul>","Our ends may be fixed while the means toward them remain chosen":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A teleological account can hold that the end toward which we move is not up to us while the route we take toward it is, which preserves a domain of choice inside a fixed order.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows that determinism about ends and freedom about means are compatible, and it is the standard medieval settlement of the problem.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aquinas holds the view in this form, with the rational end given and deliberation operating over the means. The structure recurs wherever a system fixes an objective and leaves implementation open.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a><ul><li>Deliberation is precisely the faculty this account leaves free.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a><ul><li>The teleological premise the account rests on.</li></ul></li></ul>","People often obsess over abstractions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People often obsess over what are ultimately just abstractions or mere ideals.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These abstractions/ideals often serve as significant motivating forces for people’s real life actions.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>People often follow specific dogmatic beliefs/ideologies dominated by abstractions of ideas in the world.<ul><li>This comes in the form of specific political ideologies, i.e., Marxism.</li></ul></li><li>This is also the case in some literary works, such as the Sound and the Fury, where the three Compson brothers are all obsessed with their sister Caddy, even though she has been “exiled” by the family.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This describes Mises’ Economic Calculation Problem, which states that without a market price (and thus private property) for capital goods, rational economic planning is impossible, and resources will not be optimally allocated.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most fundamental arguments against planned economic systems, since they face this problem of being unable to achieve allocative efficiency.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Prices reflect the scarcity of a resource, reflect demand for a product, and allow for the calculation of profit and loss. All these things allow people to understand if resources are being effectively allocated, and prevent waste. Prices allow you to compare things that are normally incomparable (3 hours of labor vs. 1 ton of steel), and send signals through markets about what people actually want. Prices also allow for the calculation of profit and loss, which can be used to determine if something creates or destroys value.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a><ul><li>This relates to the ideas about markets that are described in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>Another idea that relates to the concepts discussed herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>Without private property, the state has to perform economic calculation, which it cannot.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Securing a good may increase its value, but security does not necessarily mean greater value\">Securing a good may increase its value, but security does not necessarily mean greater value</a><ul><li>This describes some particularities with private property.</li></ul></li></ul>","Property rights should not be too powerful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If property rights were too powerful, then it may actually reduce the amount of development that could be done and thus lower the total level of innovation and social welfare.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues in favor of people not advocating for property rights to be made to powerful.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Estate laws in England before the Glorious Revolution meant that hereditary holdings were not able to be cultivated or split up very easily for a fear that allowing such things would lead to the gradual destruction of estates as they are simply split among the descendants or are simply squandered by one generation of the family, leaving nothing to a future generation.</li><li>Zoning laws which are too strict and which allow local homeowners to make radical decisions about the composition of their neighborhoods restricts development and thus leads to inefficiency. </li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is a difference between the idea of technical goodness and moral goodness, the former meaning being good at some skill, and the latter referring to being a good person.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important distinction to draw, not because there is technically a concrete distinction between the two concepts, but because this is a point of tension in Aristotle’s philosophy, as he seems to conflate these two types of goodness in his Ethics.</p><h4>Aristotle’s Treatment</h4><p>Aristotle argues that a good person is something who fulfills their duties/goals as a person to a satisfactory degree. However, this seems to conflate the ideas of moral goodness (i.e., being a good person) and technical goodness (i.e., being a good blacksmith). A potential solution to this is simply to reframe Aristotle’s intentions. There is no explicit discussion in Aristotle’s text that he was pursuing “good” in the strictly moral sense, and indeed, he may have just sought to find human goodness in the strictly technical sense.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>He refers to moral goodness here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The AI misalignment problem may be self-fulfilling":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The AI misalignment problem may be self-fulfilling, since the web is filled with such much information on the potential for AI-misalignment that</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that is hugely relevant for understanding and dealing with AI.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it\">Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it</a><ul><li>Perhaps this moralization of AI could lead to AI moralizing itself?</li></ul></li></ul>","The British political system has fundamentally changed since the 20th century":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The British political system is suffering from a number of problems that has fundamentally changed the way that it works compared to the government from three decades ago.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates a major change in the British political system that could potentially have lasting effects on the way that politics is conducted in the future in Britain.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>People are becoming less loyal to their party, meaning that new political parties are rising into the forefront. The traditional Labor and Conservative parties are both now trailing behind Reform UK.</li><li>The electorate is becoming more fractured and the majoritarian system means that leaders supported by only a small number of people come to power.</li><li>There are a rising number of independent political figures that become elected, who essentially run their own campaigns and also are often single-issued.<ul><li>This could leads to a Dutch-style pillarization, where parties become increasingly single-issued, or it may lead to a US style government where political outsiders are able to enter the political sphere by taking advantage of frustration.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political attitudes is one&#x27;s opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much\">Political attitudes is one's opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much</a><ul><li>British political attitudes are changing, which is leading to a period of political upset in Britain.</li></ul></li></ul><p>-</p>","The Polish economic miracle is largely a result of EU benefits and a diversified economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Polish economic miracle occurred largely as a result of EU cohesion funds, which it effectively invested into infrastructure and agriculture; the EU single market, which allowed for the free flow of goods and capital across Europe; and a diversified economy, which helped it weather tough economic storms.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Poland’s economy is as strong as it is, given its background as a former Soviet state.</p><h4>References</h4>","The Romans utilized a client state system during the Julio-Claudian era to maintain border to defense":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since the states on the borders were able to actually be turned into client states during the Julio-Claudian era, and the territories that the clients protected where the “un-Romanized” parts of the empire,  it didn’t matter much when the clients failed, allowing for this system to function.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explained success of the empire at defending its border during the Julio-Claudian era, and how even while defending vast borders, they were able to amount concentrated offensives against enemies.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the tribes that occupied the front were largely unorganized and able to be brought in as client states, this system was viable. Additionally, this represented a positive feedback loop whereby more client states meant stronger border defense/greater offensive ability, which meant the ability to accumulate even more client states.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Romans switched to a comprehensive defense strategy as centralized states developed along its borders\">The Romans switched to a comprehensive defense strategy as centralized states developed along its borders</a><ul><li>This is the result of later developments that made the defense strategy explicated in this note impossible.</li></ul></li></ul>","The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The individualistic institutions that underlie much of the Baptist sects and the Quackers (exemplified by their rejection of large scale church institutions, which even the Calvinists adopted), allowed for a development of a sense of individualism, leading to the spirit of capitalism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows that part of the development of the spirit of capitalism could be traced to the Baptist sects and the Quackers. Also, this could serve as the explanation for why the spirit of capitalism is so intense in the US.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The process of history follows naturalistic laws that can be determined by humans":"<h4>Summary</h4><h4>Significance</h4><h4>References</h4>","The profit motive sharpens incentives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The profit motive ensures that there are no perverse incentives, keep incentives sharp, to the point, and strong.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The profit motive is the key underlying lynchpin of capitalism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Friedman states the underlying principle in its general form: nobody spends money that is not their own as carefully as they spend their own. The profit motive works because it makes the spender and the residual claimant the same person, and it fails wherever that identity is broken.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying\">Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying</a><ul><li>The failure case, where the spender bears none of the cost.</li></ul></li></ul>","There are two considerations relevant to social positions, which are the existence of equal citizenship, and where someone is in the distribution of wealth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are only two considerations which are relevant when we consider how to measure people’s social positions:</p><ol><li>whether there is equal citizenship, and </li><li>where someone is in the distribution of wealth &amp; income.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>Here, Rawls specifies what exactly he is measuring when he measures the “expectations” that a representative might have.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Here, (1) is satisfied by equal basic liberties and (2) is satisfied by the second principle of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a></li></ul>","While its easier for less advanced animals to be satisfied, the more advanced can make the imperfection bearable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it can be easier for lower animals to satisfy themselves, animals with greater intelligence can make the fact that they will never be able to satisfy all of their desires bearable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains something fundamental about human existence, namely that though we may not be able to achieve complete satisfaction since we are such advanced and creatures, that very fact means that we can allow ourselves to make this incompleteness bearable.</p><h4>References</h4>"});
